      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village incorporated as an official municipality in 1893, cementing its status on Long Island’s South Shore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patchogue%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Three Patchogue churches—Congregational, United Methodist, and St. —are listed on the National Register of Historic Places, highlighting the town’s historic religious architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patchogue%2C_New_York",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Patchogue‑Medford Library showcases a mural titled “Scenes Along the Patchogue River,” painted by local artist Zoeller to celebrate the town’s waterfront.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://history.pmlib.org/patchoguemedfordareahistory/patchogue",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Patchogue’s landscape is unusually flat because glaciers never advanced that far south, leaving the area free of moraines.",
